===Etymology===
{{word|en|ine|déḱm̥}}
From {{der|en|ar|دِينَار}} and {{der|en|sh|динар}} (also from Arabic), derived from the {{der|en|la|dēnārius}}. {{doublet|en|denar|denarius|denier|diner|id4=currency|dinero|dinheiro}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdiːnɑː(ɹ)/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-dinar.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q191830}}{{lb|en|currency}} The official [[currency]] of several countries, including [[Algeria]], [[Bahrain]], [[Iraq]], [[Jordan]], [[Kuwait]], [[Libya]], [[Serbia]], [[Tunisia]] and (as ''[[denar]]'') [[North Macedonia]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-03-30|author=Simon Speakman Cordall|title=Tunisian morgue overflows as more people attempt risky sea crossing|journal=w:The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/30/migration-capital-of-the-med-hope-and-fear-on-unwelcoming-streets-of-tunisia|passage=Standing near Joseph, carrying a bag of taboon flatbreads, was Olivier, from Ivory Coast. He arrived a year ago and has been trying to save the 3,000 '''dinars''' (£780) he said it would cost to make the trip to Europe.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} An ancient [[Arab]] [[gold]] [[coin]] of 65 [[grain]]s in weight.

